1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
And now the old scout master.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
So what's the difference between your average scout patrol and a band of Desperados?

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Desperados don't have a flag that their mother sewed for them.

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Hey, this is podcast number 95.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Music.

6
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,000
This is Clark Green.

8
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,000
You may have noticed something in your retarder packet

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
And that is position code 9-2-U.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
And with this little note I delve into the extreme minutiae of scouting.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,000
But I think this is important.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
For years and years we have had a lot of people who go on to college.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:13,000
A lot of our boys go on to college.

14
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:16,000
They want to maintain their registration with the scout troop

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,000
And we usually register them as assistant scout masters.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
That's worked just fine.

17
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
But there is a new position code 9-2-U,

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
And that is the unit college scouter reserve.

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
You should be able to register anybody who is in college using the 9-2-U position code.

20
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:41,000
They will still need to take youth protection training to maintain their registration with scouts, as all adults do.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:43,000
But you know it will keep them registered

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
And they will be able to go to OA weekends and things like that.

23
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,000
And thanks to Bob White's Blather for pointing that out.

24
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
But I thought it was a useful thing and we should feature it on the podcast.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
We're going to have our third scout master panel here recording this week.

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
We've picked out a subject which is Weevilow's transition.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
We did a survey on it within the past couple of weeks over on the blog

28
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,000
And we've got a calling question and everything.

29
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:11,000
It should be great fun.

30
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
So make sure to listen to podcast 96 next week to hear the scout master panel discussion.

31
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
This time around we're going to talk about the patrol leaders council.

32
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,000
We're going to talk about the place of the patrol leaders council and the administration of a troop

33
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
and the relationship that the scout master has with the patrol leaders council

34
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
and just what we can expect from a patrol leaders council,

35
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
And we're going to do this under the heading of scout master ship in seven minutes or less.

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
If you are a long time listener to the podcast, you realize, of course,

37
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
that scout master ship under seven minutes or less.

38
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
the title of that particular feature in a podcast is aspirational.

39
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
It's hardly ever seven minutes or less.

40
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:59,000
It's usually much more than seven minutes. but I'm afraid I just like to talk.

41
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,000
So but as long as you like to listen, we'll be in good shape.

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:09,000
So let's get started, shall we scout master ship in seven minutes or less.

43
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
So what is a patrol leaders council?

44
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:19,000
How does it work?

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
How does it fit into the matrix of decision making and plan making in a Boy Scout troop?

46
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Well, it is really at the center.

47
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:31,000
It's kind of the administrative center of a scout troop,

48
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
because the patrol leaders council is going to sit down and plan

49
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and think through their program.

50
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,000
They're going to make some decisions on the content of the program,

51
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
on where they're going to go camping and the kind of activities they're going to participate in,

52
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And then they're going to take that to the troop committee

53
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:53,000
and ask for whatever kind of logistical and administrative support

54
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
they need from the troop committee to make those plans happen.

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Now that, in a nutshell, is the way that a Boy Scout troop is run.

56
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
It's run by the scouts who make these decisions,

57
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
and then it is facilitated by the committee.

58
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
So that's the place of the patrol leaders council. in the way that things happen

59
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Sounds simple, right?

60
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
It's not always quite so simple,

61
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:22,000
because one of the misunderstandings I think sometimes when we talk about a scout-led unit

62
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
or scout-led troop, is that okay, well, we're going to just give them the reins

63
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
and then we'll sit back and see what they come up with

64
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:33,000
and you know, if they decide that they want to go to the amusement park six times a year,

65
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
well, it's a boy-led troop. This is the way that it works.

66
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Well, the patrol leaders council starts with a basis.

67
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,000
They start with some parentheses around the available things that they can do

68
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and that can be various different expressions of the scout program.

69
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
The aims and methods of scouting is a good place to start.

70
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
The promises that scouting makes to a boy,

71
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
to each individual scout, is a good place to start.

72
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,000
The patrol leaders council does not have absolute carte blanche

73
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
on making decisions about activities that are not within the realm

74
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
of what they're trying to achieve as scouts.

75
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
We use as an illustration a basketball team.

76
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:17,000
If your troop was a basketball team and it was self-regulated and self-governing,

77
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
it would be done within the context of basketball.

78
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
In other words, there wouldn't be suddenly a decision from the team

79
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
that well, we're tired of basketball right now,

80
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
What we want to do is go out into the football field and start playing football.

81
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
You have to stop at some point and say,

82
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
well, I think that's a little bit beyond what we're talking about.

83
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
We are not a football team, We're a basketball team.

84
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
So we concentrate on basketball skills.

85
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
We concentrate on playing basketball games.

86
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
We play on this particular court that is marked out here on the floor.

87
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
So that is a similar way to look at a scout troop in general

88
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
and a patrol leaders council in particular.

89
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Now they have a broad spectrum of things they can choose to do,

90
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:02,000
a broad way of making decisions and doing them,

91
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
but they are bound by the scouting program

92
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:12,000
And our job as scout leaders is to help them define what the scouting program is

93
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
and to shape their activities to meet the goals of scouting.

94
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Once we understand how a patrol leaders council fits in to a troop

95
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
and then we define the playing field,

96
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
then we need to start talking about the dynamic

97
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
of having boys who are starting around age 12 up to age 17

98
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
sit down and make decisions and function as a patrol leaders council.

99
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
And there are some very practical things that have to happen there

100
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and they can be a little tricky at first

101
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:44,000
because it is not going to happen in the same way

102
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
that a similar decision-making body

103
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
that was composed strictly of adults would do business,

104
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:55,000
Because, first of all, boys at this age have a rudimentary concept

105
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:58,000
of what it means to sit down at a meeting,

106
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
make plans and decisions.

107
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:04,000
It is important for us to make sure that at least the senior patrol leader

108
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
who is going to run the patrol leaders council meetings

109
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:11,000
has a basic understanding of how to make things happen.

110
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:14,000
They need to know their place in the administration of the troop,

111
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:18,000
they need to have an idea of the field of endeavor

112
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,000
and they also need to have a way of practically arriving

113
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,000
at decisions and making plans.

114
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
You don't start out from zero and then two months later

115
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:28,000
have an effective patrol leaders council.

116
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,000
It just doesn't work that way.

117
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Patrol leaders councils develop over time

118
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:35,000
their procedures, their methods, the way that they do things

119
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:37,000
and the way that it is looked on in the troop

120
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:39,000
amongst the scouts and the adults-

121
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
the adults being a very important aspect of this-

122
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:45,000
that develops over time.

123
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:48,000
And then, as soon as you have a patrol leaders council

124
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
that is pretty efficient and doing well

125
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:52,000
and making decisions- guess what?

126
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,000
It's time for the next patrol leaders council to take over.

127
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
So let's talk for a moment just about the transition

128
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
that's going to have to be made in adult thinking

129
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
to get a good, effective patrol leaders council off the ground.

130
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:06,000
There are a number of troops out there

131
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:09,000
whose decisions are made by the adults

132
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:11,000
so far as what the program will be

133
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,000
and what the arrangements will be

134
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:16,000
and where they will go camping,

135
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:21,000
what activities they will pursue and so forth.

136
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
Sometimes that's made at the committee level,

137
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
Sometimes that's made by the scout master,

138
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
or sometimes it's made by a combination of the two.

139
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Well, I have to say that that is not

140
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
what the intention of scouting is.

141
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:36,000
The intention of scouting is not for a program of activities

142
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:38,000
to be planned by adults and presented to boys.

143
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:41,000
The intention of scouting is for boys to plan

144
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
their own activities, to choose what they want to do,

145
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,000
to choose what they want to pursue

146
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,000
within the paradigm of scouting, within, you know,

147
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:51,000
as I said earlier, that basketball court

148
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:53,000
that's marked off on the floor

149
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:56,000
And it can be quite challenging to arrive at a place

150
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
in the minds of the adults who are involved with the troop

151
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
that this is a good thing to do.

152
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Usually, the first argument is that, well,

153
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,000
if we give them this decision-making power,

154
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
they will just make decisions that we don't agree with

155
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,000
And that would be bad.

156
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:12,000
Well, first of all, you don't get to give them

157
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
the decision-making power. Scouting does-

158
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:19,000
The scout program already puts the decision-making power

159
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
in the hands of the patrol leader's counsel,

160
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
not the committee and not the scout master.

161
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:25,000
That's the way that it is.

162
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
You've improperly usurped that authority from them.

163
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
if you're making the decisions about, you know

164
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
camping and things like that,

165
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,000
This is a fine point. so I'm going to say it again:

166
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
There's a basketball court marked off on the floor.

167
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:42,000
There are a set of things that scouts do.

168
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:47,000
There's a field of activity and endeavor that scouts follow

169
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
And I'll repeat myself:

170
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
I am not saying that the patrol leader's counsel

171
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
gets to step out of bounds on that stuff.

172
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:54,000
They don't.

173
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
They need to stay in the playing field, very plainly.

174
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
But they have the ability to choose and to plan

175
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:04,000
And it's not necessarily the choices they make,

176
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
It's not necessarily the plans that they make.

177
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:12,000
It's the process of having this happen that is important.

178
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
I will say that again.

179
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
It's not necessarily the plans that they make,

180
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
It's not necessarily the choices of activities that they make.

181
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:24,000
It is the process of having them do that.

182
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
That is the biggest part of leadership

183
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
and character development in scouting

184
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:31,000
that we get out of using the patrol leader's counsel,

185
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
The process of it,

186
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:37,000
When they are sitting in this kind of little representative

187
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
government where the patrol leaders

188
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
are supposed to be talking to the scouts

189
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
about what the scouts would like to do

190
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
And they take that to the patrol leader's counsel

191
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and they discuss it and they make a final decision

192
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
about what will happen next month

193
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,000
or what will happen, you know, for the annual calendar,

194
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
And then they take that to the troop committee

195
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
who's going to give them administrative

196
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and logistical support to make their plans real.

197
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:08,000
That's an incredibly wonderful process

198
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,000
for a Scout-age boy to participate in.

199
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
He learns a lot about life

200
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and his character and leadership skills

201
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,000
are built through participating in that process.

202
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
Now, what I hear many times is:

203
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:25,000
okay. well, we gave this patrol leader's counsel thing

204
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
and this youth-led thing an opportunity,

205
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
We gave it a chance and for a couple of months

206
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
we had a patrol leader's counsel meeting

207
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
and we did it just like you said

208
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and it just didn't work. and so you know

209
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:40,000
that obviously cannot be the way to go.

210
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:43,000
Well, it doesn't happen in a couple of months.

211
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
as I said earlier,

212
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
It can be a long-trying process.

213
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
to get this to gel

214
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,000
and to get your youth members

215
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,000
to understand exactly how this is going to work.

216
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,000
You're going to be coaching and training

217
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
and mentoring and encouraging and cajoling.

218
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:05,000
You're going to be looking for the least little bit of initiative

219
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
and you're going to build on it

220
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
Now. you cannot expect a bunch of boys,

221
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:15,000
given you know the outline of what a patrol leader's counsel is,

222
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
to go and sit in a room

223
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
and to, in an hour later,

224
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
come out with a perfectly balanced plan

225
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
of activities and camping

226
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
and things like that. that is just.

227
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
it's ready to publish and put on a calendar.

228
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
That's not going to happen.

229
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
You can expect that after you've built on it

230
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
for a couple of years,

231
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
after you've worked with you know

232
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
the senior patrol leader that you have now

233
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
and the next one and the next one,

234
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
and you've seen how the process works

235
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and you keep them moving

236
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
and forging ahead in it,

237
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,000
then you'll get the effective plans and things.

238
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
But with boys

239
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
it will be led to this point by point,

240
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:56,000
piece by piece, until they begin to see

241
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
exactly what the big picture is.

242
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Now I go through this process

243
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
on an annual basis with each new senior patrol leader.

244
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Think about this:

245
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
My senior patrol leaders are usually elected

246
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
from someone who has sat on that patrol leader's counsel

247
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
for at least one year.

248
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
He'll either be a former patrol leader

249
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
or a former assistant senior patrol leader.

250
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
That's just usually who the boys elect-

251
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
And even though he's been in on this process

252
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
for at least a year,

253
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
sometimes two, sometimes three years.

254
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
he still doesn't have the concept

255
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:32,000
of how important his planning is

256
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
and how important it is for him

257
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
to have a depth of planning.

258
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
None of them have that concept

259
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
when they start the job.

260
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
It only builds over time

261
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
And I don't go beat him over the head

262
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
every time that there's some little slip-up

263
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
or failure or something like that.

264
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
I just

265
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Here's with my present senior patrol leader,

266
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
who's a very sharp guy

267
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
and who's doing a wonderful job.

268
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
Just every once in a while

269
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
I see him standing in the middle of a room

270
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
or a campsite with this kind of look on his face-

271
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
What the world is going on,

272
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
And I'll stand next to him.

273
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
I'll say it's not as easy as it looks, is it?

274
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:15,000
And we kind of have a chuckle and smile

275
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
And I say, look, you're doing fine.

276
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
You. just

277
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
What do you need to think about right now?

278
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
And we'll have a quick discussion

279
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
and he'll move ahead.

280
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:28,000
And this is small, incremental steps.

281
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
It's things that we need to exercise

282
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
a great deal of patience with

283
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
and that we need to have

284
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
a realistic expectation for

285
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
Now, the other part of our job,

286
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
other than training and coaching

287
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
and mentoring our senior patrol leader

288
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
and the members of the patrol leader council

289
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
is to make sure that we are preserving

290
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
the field of play.

291
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
And what I mean by that is:

292
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
let's go back to the basketball analogy.

293
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Let's say we have a basketball team

294
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
and they're not a great team.

295
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
Okay, I mean, let's be honest,

296
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
We've been,

297
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
We're a few weeks into the season

298
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
and they're having fun playing and stuff like that,

299
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
but they're not making many baskets

300
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
and they're not winning games.

301
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
Well, I'm going to be on the sidelines

302
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
with my coaching staff

303
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
and they're going to be getting a little impatient

304
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
because they want to see more action,

305
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
They want to see some numbers on the board.

306
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Can my coaching staff and I

307
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
step over the sideline

308
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
into the field of play,

309
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
grab the basketball

310
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and start making hoops for our team?

311
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Can that happen?

312
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
Here's the way the illustration applies

313
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
to our scout troop

314
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and our patrol leaders council.

315
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
We're going to have the patrol leaders council meeting

316
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
and the boys are going to sit there

317
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
and they're going to talk about what happened

318
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
in school the other day

319
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
and that show on TV in this game

320
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
or this app from my phone,

321
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and they're finally going to start talking

322
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
about a camping trip or a meeting

323
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
or something like that.

324
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
They will plan at the speed of thought.

325
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
What are we going to do next Monday night?

326
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Oh, we're going to do knots. Great,

327
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
Good, Write that down. We're going to do knots.

328
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
So what's next?

329
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
We're going to be there

330
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and we'll get aggravated by that

331
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and then when those plans

332
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
that are kind of incomplete

333
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
don't happen properly,

334
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
the other adults involved with us

335
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
are going to get a little aggravated by that

336
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
and we're going to wonder what in the world we're doing

337
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:28,000
and whether or not this is a very good idea.

338
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
Well, we have to overcome that aggravation

339
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
by looking at the process involved.

340
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
So let's say that my patrol leaders, council,

341
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
are not exactly that.

342
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
They sit down and they do. what I say

343
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
is planning at the speed of thought.

344
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
If they think it's planned, it's done,

345
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
Let's move on.

346
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
So they say next week we're going to do knots.

347
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
They show up next week

348
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
and everybody's kind of wandering around

349
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
and I talk to the senior patrol there for a moment.

350
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
I say what's on the boards for this week?

351
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
What are you planning to do?

352
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Well, we're going to do knots.

353
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Okay, great, Which knots are you going to do?

354
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Well, just, you know

355
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
we're going to do knots.

356
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
I say: well, which one's exactly?

357
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
I maybe a square knot.

358
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
Well, that sounds great.

359
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
Now, what are you going to need to do that?

360
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:18,000
I guess we're going to need some rope or something.

361
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Well, that's right.

362
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
Now, who's supposed to get the rope?

363
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
I guess I could.

364
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
Well, if you go do that,

365
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
you're going to have your head down getting rope

366
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
and everybody else is going to be running around

367
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
like they are right now.

368
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
I'm going to do better for that.

369
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
So do you understand the process here?

370
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
Now, I maybe haven't waited

371
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
until that meeting happens.

372
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
Maybe I'll catch them at the senior,

373
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
at the patrol leaders council meeting,

374
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and I'll ask a few of these questions

375
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
and that will get them to thinking

376
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
and they'll say, oh, we have to do more than just

377
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
think about this.

378
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
We actually have to really think about it

379
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
and we have to plan things out

380
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
So you can convince them

381
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
some of the times to think about stuff.

382
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Some of the times you'll ask questions

383
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and they'll say, oh, we'll just take care of it,

384
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and then it'll fall flat.

385
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and then you have little reflection

386
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
and you say,

387
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
so, you really should have done this.

388
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
You probably didn't think about that,

389
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and

390
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
how is that going to inform

391
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
what you do the next time.

392
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
So you build on little bits of initiative

393
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
step by step by step.

394
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Now we've talked

395
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
about the philosophic underpinnings

396
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
and

397
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
some of the practical things

398
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
about a patrol leaders council,

399
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
but let's talk a little bit about

400
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
the very practical things

401
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
of the way that it works,

402
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
and in doing this I'm going to describe

403
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
how I manage this.

404
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
Now you might find this an effective model

405
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
for you.

406
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
I'm not saying that it is the be all

407
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and end all in the way that you absolutely

408
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
have to do things, because

409
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
it's not described in scouting literature

410
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
as this way or anything like that.

411
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Here's the way that I have been

412
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
effectively able to work with

413
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
my youth in putting

414
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
a patrol leaders council together

415
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
that, reasonably, that plans

416
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
and makes things happen.

417
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
First of all, we need to talk about

418
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
who meets with the patrol leaders council.

419
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
You cannot

420
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
have six adults in the room

421
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
with a patrol leaders council

422
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
unless they are

423
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
incredibly patient, silent adults

424
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
or they're simply to observe.

425
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
If they're able to do that, that's fine.

426
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
They can be in the room,

427
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
But remember, with them in the room.

428
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
it changes the entire thing.

429
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
It changes the way that boys talk

430
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
to each other. It changes

431
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
the things that they say.

432
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
It changes the way that they approach

433
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
problems because they know everybody

434
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
is looking and listening. This can be useful

435
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
sometimes, but most of the time

436
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
it's not going to be very useful.

437
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
I am the only person in the room

438
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
with a scout

439
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
for a patrol leaders council meeting.

440
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
The way that I handle it

441
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
is I will be there for the first

442
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
two or three minutes and the last

443
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
five minutes.

444
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
Apart from that, I won't even be

445
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
in the room.

446
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
The first two or three minutes I will

447
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
speak with the senior patrol leader

448
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
in the presence of the other scouts

449
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
and I will ask him what he hopes to

450
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
accomplish at this meeting.

451
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
I will ask him if he has any questions

452
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
and usually he will say no.

453
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
Then I will excuse myself,

454
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
saying I will be across the hall.

455
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
if you should need anything

456
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
or you have any questions,

457
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
Definitely give me a call

458
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
When you are done with your meeting.

459
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
I would like to have the last five minutes

460
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
to ask a few questions myself.

